1
00:00:04,638 --> 00:00:10,317
It was clear that I must repair to our town next day, and in the first flow of my repentance,

2
00:00:10,317 --> 00:00:15,838
it was equally clear that I must stay at Joe’s. But, when I had secured my box-place

3
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:20,599
by to-morrow’s coach, and had been down to Mr. Pocket’s and back, I was not by any means

4
00:00:20,599 --> 00:00:25,559
convinced on the last point, and began to invent reasons and make excuses for putting up at the

5
00:00:25,559 --> 00:00:34,039
Blue Boar. I should be an inconvenience at Joe’s; I was not expected, and my bed would not be ready;

6
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:39,317
I should be too far from Miss Havisham’s, and she was exacting and mightn’t like it.

7
00:00:39,317 --> 00:00:44,199
All other swindlers upon earth are nothing to the self-swindlers, and with such pretences did

8
00:00:44,199 --> 00:00:50,958
I cheat myself. Surely a curious thing. That I should innocently take a bad half-crown of

9
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:56,520
somebody else’s manufacture is reasonable enough; but that I should knowingly reckon the spurious

10
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:03,438
coin of my own make as good money! An obliging stranger, under pretence of compactly folding

11
00:01:03,438 --> 00:01:09,718
up my bank-notes for security’s sake, abstracts the notes and gives me nutshells; but what is

12
00:01:09,719 --> 00:01:15,919
his sleight of hand to mine, when I fold up my own nutshells and pass them on myself as notes!

13
00:01:15,919 --> 00:01:19,599
Having settled that I must go to the Blue Boar, my mind was much disturbed

14
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:24,317
by indecision whether or not to take the Avenger. It was tempting to think of that

15
00:01:24,317 --> 00:01:29,919
expensive Mercenary publicly airing his boots in the archway of the Blue Boar’s posting-yard;

16
00:01:29,919 --> 00:01:34,438
it was almost solemn to imagine him casually produced in the tailor’s shop,

17
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:40,118
and confounding the disrespectful senses of Trabb’s boy. On the other hand,

18
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:46,798
Trabb’s boy might worm himself into his intimacy and tell him things; or, reckless and desperate

19
00:01:46,798 --> 00:01:52,520
wretch as I knew he could be, might hoot him in the High Street. My patroness, too,

20
00:01:52,520 --> 00:02:00,199
might hear of him, and not approve. On the whole, I resolved to leave the Avenger behind.

21
00:02:00,199 --> 00:02:06,039
It was the afternoon coach by which I had taken my place, and, as winter had now come round,

22
00:02:06,039 --> 00:02:11,798
I should not arrive at my destination until two or three hours after dark.

23
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:16,680
Our time of starting from the Cross Keys was two o’clock. I arrived on

24
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:22,238
the ground with a quarter of an hour to spare, attended by the Avenger,—if I may

25
00:02:22,238 --> 00:02:27,679
connect that expression with one who never attended on me if he could possibly help it.

26
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,520
At that time it was customary to carry Convicts down to the dock-yards by

27
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:37,199
stage-coach. As I had often heard of them in the capacity of outside passengers,

28
00:02:37,199 --> 00:02:42,519
and had more than once seen them on the high road dangling their ironed legs over the coach roof,

29
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,958
I had no cause to be surprised when Herbert, meeting me in the yard,

30
00:02:45,958 --> 00:02:50,958
came up and told me there were two convicts going down with me. But I had a reason that

31
00:02:50,958 --> 00:02:57,278
was an old reason now for constitutionally faltering whenever I heard the word “convict.”

32
00:02:57,278 --> 00:02:59,679
“You don’t mind them, Handel?” said Herbert.

33
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,000
“O no!”

34
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,958
“I thought you seemed as if you didn’t like them?”

35
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:06,759
“I can’t pretend that I do like them, and I suppose

36
00:03:06,759 --> 00:03:10,520
you don’t particularly. But I don’t mind them.”

37
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:18,638
“See! There they are,” said Herbert, “coming out of the Tap. What a degraded and vile sight it is!”

38
00:03:18,638 --> 00:03:23,118
They had been treating their guard, I suppose, for they had a gaoler with them, and all three came

39
00:03:23,118 --> 00:03:29,477
out wiping their mouths on their hands. The two convicts were handcuffed together, and had irons

40
00:03:29,478 --> 00:03:36,238
on their legs,—irons of a pattern that I knew well. They wore the dress that I likewise knew

41
00:03:36,238 --> 00:03:43,278
well. Their keeper had a brace of pistols, and carried a thick-knobbed bludgeon under his arm;

42
00:03:43,278 --> 00:03:47,879
but he was on terms of good understanding with them, and stood with them beside him,

43
00:03:47,879 --> 00:03:53,680
looking on at the putting-to of the horses, rather with an air as if the convicts were an interesting

44
00:03:53,680 --> 00:04:01,000
Exhibition not formally open at the moment, and he the Curator. One was a taller and stouter man than

45
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,159
the other, and appeared as a matter of course, according to the mysterious ways of the world,

46
00:04:06,159 --> 00:04:12,598
both convict and free, to have had allotted to him the smaller suit of clothes. His arms and

47
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:19,438
legs were like great pincushions of those shapes, and his attire disguised him absurdly; but I knew

48
00:04:19,438 --> 00:04:25,519
his half-closed eye at one glance. There stood the man whom I had seen on the settle at the

49
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:32,840
Three Jolly Bargemen on a Saturday night, and who had brought me down with his invisible gun!

50
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:40,080
It was easy to make sure that as yet he knew me no more than if he had never seen me in his life.

51
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:46,680
He looked across at me, and his eye appraised my watch-chain, and then he incidentally spat

52
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:51,520
and said something to the other convict, and they laughed and slued themselves round with

53
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:58,118
a clink of their coupling manacle, and looked at something else. The great numbers on their backs,

54
00:04:58,118 --> 00:05:04,958
as if they were street doors; their coarse mangy ungainly outer surface, as if they were lower

55
00:05:04,958 --> 00:05:11,317
animals; their ironed legs, apologetically garlanded with pocket-handkerchiefs;

56
00:05:11,317 --> 00:05:16,919
and the way in which all present looked at them and kept from them; made them (as Herbert had

57
00:05:16,919 --> 00:05:24,278
said) a most disagreeable and degraded spectacle. But this was not the worst of it. It came out

58
00:05:24,278 --> 00:05:29,080
that the whole of the back of the coach had been taken by a family removing from London,

59
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:33,398
and that there were no places for the two prisoners but on the seat in front behind

60
00:05:33,398 --> 00:05:40,118
the coachman. Hereupon, a choleric gentleman, who had taken the fourth place on that seat,

61
00:05:40,118 --> 00:05:45,558
flew into a most violent passion, and said that it was a breach of contract to mix him up with such

62
00:05:45,559 --> 00:05:51,520
villainous company, and that it was poisonous, and pernicious, and infamous, and shameful,

63
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:58,199
and I don’t know what else. At this time the coach was ready and the coachman impatient,

64
00:05:58,199 --> 00:06:02,039
and we were all preparing to get up, and the prisoners had come over with their

65
00:06:02,039 --> 00:06:08,719
keeper,—bringing with them that curious flavour of bread-poultice, baize, rope-yarn, and hearthstone,

66
00:06:08,719 --> 00:06:13,278
which attends the convict presence. “Don’t take it so much amiss,

67
00:06:13,278 --> 00:06:18,438
sir,” pleaded the keeper to the angry passenger; “I’ll sit next you myself. I’ll put ’em on the

68
00:06:18,438 --> 00:06:23,958
outside of the row. They won’t interfere with you, sir. You needn’t know they’re there.”

69
00:06:23,958 --> 00:06:28,739
“And don’t blame me,” growled the convict I had recognised. “I don’t want to go. I

70
00:06:28,739 --> 00:06:35,080
am quite ready to stay behind. As fur as I am concerned any one’s welcome to my place.”

71
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:41,438
“Or mine,” said the other, gruffly. “I wouldn’t have incommoded none of you, if I’d had my way.”

72
00:06:41,438 --> 00:06:46,958
Then they both laughed, and began cracking nuts, and spitting the shells about.—As I

73
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:52,559
really think I should have liked to do myself, if I had been in their place and so despised.

74
00:06:52,559 --> 00:06:56,759
At length, it was voted that there was no help for the angry gentleman, and that he must either

75
00:06:56,759 --> 00:07:03,080
go in his chance company or remain behind. So he got into his place, still making complaints,

76
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:07,519
and the keeper got into the place next him, and the convicts hauled themselves up as well as

77
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:13,638
they could, and the convict I had recognised sat behind me with his breath on the hair of my head.

78
00:07:13,638 --> 00:07:19,238
“Good-bye, Handel!” Herbert called out as we started. I thought what a

79
00:07:19,238 --> 00:07:24,039
blessed fortune it was, that he had found another name for me than Pip.

80
00:07:24,039 --> 00:07:28,759
It is impossible to express with what acuteness I felt the convict’s breathing,

81
00:07:28,759 --> 00:07:34,520
not only on the back of my head, but all along my spine. The sensation was like being touched

82
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:40,759
in the marrow with some pungent and searching acid, it set my very teeth on edge. He seemed to

83
00:07:40,759 --> 00:07:46,199
have more breathing business to do than another man, and to make more noise in doing it; and I

84
00:07:46,199 --> 00:07:52,519
was conscious of growing high-shouldered on one side, in my shrinking endeavours to fend him off.

85
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:58,159
The weather was miserably raw, and the two cursed the cold. It made us all lethargic

86
00:07:58,159 --> 00:08:02,278
before we had gone far, and when we had left the Half-way House behind,

87
00:08:02,278 --> 00:08:08,317
we habitually dozed and shivered and were silent. I dozed off, myself,

88
00:08:08,317 --> 00:08:12,317
in considering the question whether I ought to restore a couple of pounds sterling to this

89
00:08:12,317 --> 00:08:18,080
creature before losing sight of him, and how it could best be done. In the act of dipping forward

90
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:24,359
as if I were going to bathe among the horses, I woke in a fright and took the question up again.

91
00:08:24,359 --> 00:08:29,238
But I must have lost it longer than I had thought, since, although I could recognise

92
00:08:29,238 --> 00:08:35,080
nothing in the darkness and the fitful lights and shadows of our lamps, I traced marsh country

93
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:41,759
in the cold damp wind that blew at us. Cowering forward for warmth and to make me a screen

94
00:08:41,759 --> 00:08:47,879
against the wind, the convicts were closer to me than before. The very first words I heard

95
00:08:47,879 --> 00:08:55,438
them interchange as I became conscious, were the words of my own thought, “Two One Pound notes.”

96
00:08:55,438 --> 00:08:59,159
“How did he get ’em?” said the convict I had never seen.

97
00:08:59,159 --> 00:09:02,719
“How should I know?” returned the other. “He had ’em stowed

98
00:09:02,719 --> 00:09:07,000
away somehows. Giv him by friends, I expect.”

99
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,278
“I wish,” said the other, with a bitter curse upon the cold, “that I had ’em here.”

100
00:09:13,278 --> 00:09:16,477
“Two one pound notes, or friends?”

101
00:09:16,478 --> 00:09:20,398
“Two one pound notes. I’d sell all the friends I ever had for one,

102
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:25,118
and think it a blessed good bargain. Well? So he says—?”

103
00:09:25,118 --> 00:09:29,958
“So he says,” resumed the convict I had recognised,—“it was all said and done in

104
00:09:29,958 --> 00:09:34,559
half a minute, behind a pile of timber in the Dock-yard,—‘You’re a-going to be discharged?’

105
00:09:35,559 --> 00:09:40,359
Yes, I was. Would I find out that boy that had fed him and kep his secret,

106
00:09:40,359 --> 00:09:45,000
and give him them two one pound notes? Yes, I would. And I did.”

107
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,559
“More fool you,” growled the other. “I’d have spent ’em on a Man, in wittles and

108
00:09:49,559 --> 00:09:53,919
drink. He must have been a green one. Mean to say he knowed nothing of you?”

109
00:09:53,919 --> 00:09:57,719
“Not a ha’porth. Different gangs and different ships. He was tried

110
00:09:57,719 --> 00:10:01,559
again for prison breaking, and got made a Lifer.”

111
00:10:01,559 --> 00:10:06,840
“And was that—Honour!—the only time you worked out, in this part of the country?”

112
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:08,638
“The only time.”

113
00:10:08,638 --> 00:10:11,118
“What might have been your opinion of the place?”

114
00:10:11,118 --> 00:10:18,798
“A most beastly place. Mudbank, mist, swamp, and work; work, swamp, mist, and mudbank.”

115
00:10:18,798 --> 00:10:23,919
They both execrated the place in very strong language, and gradually growled themselves out,

116
00:10:23,919 --> 00:10:28,759
and had nothing left to say. After overhearing this dialogue,

117
00:10:28,759 --> 00:10:34,199
I should assuredly have got down and been left in the solitude and darkness of the highway, but

118
00:10:34,199 --> 00:10:40,637
for feeling certain that the man had no suspicion of my identity. Indeed, I was not only so changed

119
00:10:40,638 --> 00:10:45,520
in the course of nature, but so differently dressed and so differently circumstanced,

120
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:50,317
that it was not at all likely he could have known me without accidental help. Still,

121
00:10:50,317 --> 00:10:55,118
the coincidence of our being together on the coach, was sufficiently strange to fill me with

122
00:10:55,118 --> 00:11:02,477
a dread that some other coincidence might at any moment connect me, in his hearing, with my name.

123
00:11:02,478 --> 00:11:08,359
For this reason, I resolved to alight as soon as we touched the town, and put myself out of his

124
00:11:08,359 --> 00:11:16,118
hearing. This device I executed successfully. My little portmanteau was in the boot under my feet;

125
00:11:16,118 --> 00:11:22,519
I had but to turn a hinge to get it out; I threw it down before me, got down after it,

126
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:28,599
and was left at the first lamp on the first stones of the town pavement. As to the convicts, they

127
00:11:28,599 --> 00:11:34,439
went their way with the coach, and I knew at what point they would be spirited off to the river.

128
00:11:34,958 --> 00:11:39,879
In my fancy, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting for them at the slime-washed

129
00:11:39,879 --> 00:11:47,879
stairs,—again heard the gruff “Give way, you!” like and order to dogs,—again saw the wicked

130
00:11:47,879 --> 00:11:53,919
Noah’s Ark lying out on the black water. I could not have said what I was afraid of,

131
00:11:53,919 --> 00:12:00,398
for my fear was altogether undefined and vague, but there was great fear upon me. As I walked

132
00:12:00,398 --> 00:12:06,159
on to the hotel, I felt that a dread, much exceeding the mere apprehension of a painful

133
00:12:06,159 --> 00:12:13,159
or disagreeable recognition, made me tremble. I am confident that it took no distinctness of shape,

134
00:12:13,159 --> 00:12:18,000
and that it was the revival for a few minutes of the terror of childhood.

135
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,438
The coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not only ordered my dinner there,

136
00:12:22,438 --> 00:12:27,679
but had sat down to it, before the waiter knew me. As soon as he had apologised for

137
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:33,278
the remissness of his memory, he asked me if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook?

138
00:12:33,278 --> 00:12:36,039
“No,” said I, “certainly not.”

139
00:12:36,039 --> 00:12:40,477
The waiter (it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials, on

140
00:12:40,478 --> 00:12:45,478
the day when I was bound) appeared surprised, and took the earliest opportunity of putting a dirty

141
00:12:45,478 --> 00:12:52,398
old copy of a local newspaper so directly in my way, that I took it up and read this paragraph:—

142
00:12:52,398 --> 00:12:57,679
Our readers will learn, not altogether without interest, in reference to the recent romantic

143
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:03,238
rise in fortune of a young artificer in iron of this neighbourhood (what a theme,

144
00:13:03,238 --> 00:13:08,477
by the way, for the magic pen of our as yet not universally acknowledged townsman Tooby,

145
00:13:08,478 --> 00:13:13,680
the poet of our columns!) that the youth’s earliest patron, companion, and friend,

146
00:13:13,680 --> 00:13:19,398
was a highly respected individual not entirely unconnected with the corn and seed trade, and

147
00:13:19,398 --> 00:13:24,718
whose eminently convenient and commodious business premises are situate within a hundred miles of the

148
00:13:24,719 --> 00:13:31,159
High Street. It is not wholly irrespective of our personal feelings that we record him as the Mentor

149
00:13:31,159 --> 00:13:36,558
of our young Telemachus, for it is good to know that our town produced the founder of the latter’s

150
00:13:36,559 --> 00:13:44,478
fortunes. Does the thought-contracted brow of the local Sage or the lustrous eye of local Beauty

151
00:13:44,478 --> 00:13:52,458
inquire whose fortunes? We believe that Quintin Matsys was the Blacksmith of Antwerp. Verbum sap.

152
00:13:52,458 --> 00:13:53,238
-

153
00:13:53,238 --> 00:13:58,840
I entertain a conviction, based upon large experience, that if in the days of my prosperity

154
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:04,559
I had gone to the North Pole, I should have met somebody there, wandering Esquimaux or civilized

155
00:14:04,559 --> 00:14:09,359
man, who would have told me that Pumblechook was my earliest patron and the founder of

156
00:14:09,359 --> 00:14:11,000
my fortunes. Chapter 29.
